# Recipes

<p class="lede">
Each recipe is a whole plugin, verified on a dev daemon with the commands on its page. The code shown is the code
that is checked: pages read it from the repository at build time.
</p>

- **[Timeline renderer](/docs/recipes/timeline-renderer)**: a card under every agent turn that used tools, and a
  screen listing all turns. Uses `server.on("agent.turn_ended")`, `timeline.append`, `addTimelineRenderer`,
  `addScreen` and an RPC.
- **[Settings screen](/docs/recipes/settings)**: a settings screen whose values the server reads when a turn ends.
  Uses `defineSettings`, `registerSettings`, `useSettings`, `addSettingsScreen` and `addCommandCenterItem`.
- **[Server hooks](/docs/recipes/server-hooks)**: an MCP server injected into every new agent, and a log line for
  every finished turn. Uses `server.before("agent.create")` and `server.on("agent.turn_ended")`.

The timeline renderer is the `agent-hook` template of `ppd create`, which the devkit's own tests create, check and
run. The other two live in
[`docs-site/recipes`](https://github.com/nkwib/paseo-plugin-devkit/tree/main/docs-site/recipes), and CI runs
`ppd check` on them.

<h2 id="try">Try one</h2>

The settings and server hooks recipes are in the repository, not in the npm package (the timeline renderer is
`ppd create --template agent-hook`). Clone the repository, then run from any plugin repo with `paseo-plugin-devkit`
installed and a dev daemon running:

```sh
npx ppd check path/to/recipes/settings
npx ppd install path/to/recipes/settings
```

A recipe's devDependencies are there for types and tests only: Paseo provides `@getpaseo/plugin`, `react`,
`react-native` and `zod` at runtime, so a recipe installs without `npm install`.
